    "result_text": "The build is green. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Change: address normalization on owner creation\n\nThe `POST /api/owners` pipeline now normalizes the address as the first thing it does, and every address comparison uses that normalized form.\n\n**New shared utility** \u2014 `OwnerAddress.java` (in the `function/owner` package, alongside the existing `OwnerTelephone`/`OwnerEmail` helpers):\n- `normalize(String)`: trims and collapses internal whitespace, upper-cases, and expands whole-token abbreviations `ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`. Idempotent; `null` stays `null`, all-whitespace \u2192 `\"\"`.\n- `normalizeForCompare(String)`: same, but maps `null` to `\"\"` for safe equality checks.\n\n**`ValidateOwnerFields`** \u2014 normalizes the address *before* the required-field check, then stores the normalized value back on the request. Because it happens first, an address that is blank only after normalization (e.g. `\"   \"`) is now caught by the existing `require(...)` check \u2192 400. Since the request is republished as a variable, `BuildOwner` maps the normalized value onto the entity, so it's persisted and returned as `address`.\n\n**`CheckUniqueHousehold`** \u2014 household duplicate detection now compares addresses via `OwnerAddress.normalizeForCompare` (last-name comparison unchanged: case-insensitive, whitespace-collapsed).\n\n**`AssignHousehold`** \u2014 both the membership match and the derived shared `householdId` now use the normalized address, so joining owners compute the same id.\n\nJavadocs updated to describe the normalized comparison.\n\n**Tests:** the 3 acceptance tests (`coreNormalizesWhitespaceAndCase`, `functionalityExpandsAbbreviations`, `errorRejectsBlankAfterNormalize`) and the full suite pass \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\nNote: the update pipeline (`PUT /api/owners/{ownerId}` via `ApplyOwner`) was left untouched, since the spec scopes normalization to owner *creation*.",
